            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(V\\) the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nn-V\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a column \\(x=i\\) does not contain a vertical line, each point of that column must be covered by a line that is not vertical.  \nA non‑vertical line meets a fixed column in at most one point; therefore covering the whole column requires at least as many non‑vertical lines as the column contains points, i.e. at least \\(n+1-i\\) such lines.\n\nLet \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of columns without a vertical line.  \nThe total number of non‑vertical lines is \\(n-V\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nn-V\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{i\\in C}(n+1-i). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|C|=m\\), the right‑hand side of (1) is minimized when the missing columns are the rightmost ones, namely \\(i=n-m+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nIn that case  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in C}(n+1-i)=1+2+\\dots +m=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m=n-V\\), inequality (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\nm\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and rearranging gives \\(m(m-1)\\le0\\), whence \\(m\\le1\\).  \nThus \\(n-V=m\\le1\\), i.e. at most one column lacks a vertical line. ∎\n\nBy symmetry the same statement holds for horizontal lines (rows) and for lines of slope \\(-1\\) (anti‑diagonals).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\;\nH=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\;\nD=\\#\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nV+H+D+s=n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma 1 applied to the three families we obtain\n\n* at most one column is without a vertical line,\n* at most one row is without a horizontal line,\n* at most one anti‑diagonal is without a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nConsequently the set of non‑sunny lines can contain **at most one** line that is not vertical; otherwise we would have at least two rows or two anti‑diagonals missing, contradicting Lemma 1.  \nHence the number of non‑vertical non‑sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn-V\\le 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad V\\ge n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2) together with (3) yields  \n\n\\[\nH+D+s=n-V\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(H,D\\ge0\\), we have \\(s\\le1\\).  \nTherefore a covering with \\(n\\) lines can contain **at most one sunny line**.  \nIn particular, configurations with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines do **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n#### \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n#### \\(k=1\\)\n\nFor any \\(n\\ge3\\) consider the following family of \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{vertical: }x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 \\quad (n-2\\text{ lines}),\\\\\n&\\text{horizontal: }y=1 \\quad (1\\text{ line}),\\\\\n&\\text{sunny: }S:\\;y = x-(n-3) \\quad (1\\text{ line}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S\\) equals \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(S\\) is sunny and distinct from the other lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le2\\).  \n  – For \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n  – For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(2 = (n-1)-(n-3)\\), so \\((n-1,2)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family, and exactly one line (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny. Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe counting argument (Lemma 1) forces at least \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines to be vertical; consequently at most one line can be sunny.  \nBoth possibilities \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realised by explicit constructions.  \n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
