            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are **non‑sunny** and belong to exactly one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with two sunny lines is impossible\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains at least two sunny lines.  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nv=|\\mathcal L\\cap\\mathcal V|,\\;\nh=|\\mathcal L\\cap\\mathcal H|,\\;\nd=|\\mathcal L\\cap\\mathcal D|,\\;\ns=|\\mathcal L\\setminus(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D)|\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d+s=n,\\qquad s\\ge 2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause each sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\), a sunny line meets a fixed column \\(x=i\\) in **exactly one** point, and the same holds for rows and for anti‑diagonals.\n\nSince \\(s\\ge2\\), from (1) we obtain \\(v\\le n-2\\); consequently at least two columns of the triangle contain **no** vertical line.  \nThe two right‑most columns are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{n-1}&=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\\\\nC_{n}&=\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll other columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) are equipped with a vertical line and are therefore completely covered.\n\nThe only lines that can meet the three points of \\(C_{n-1}\\cup C_{n}\\) are the two sunny lines, because the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are already used as vertical lines (there is no horizontal or diagonal line left).  \nA sunny line can intersect a given column in at most one point; hence each of the two sunny lines can cover at most one of the two points of \\(C_{n-1}\\).  \nThus the two sunny lines together can cover at most two of the three points \\((n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\), leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction to the covering property.\n\nTherefore a covering with \\(s\\ge2\\) sunny lines does **not** exist, i.e.\n\n\\[\ns\\le 1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{vertical lines }x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 \\quad (n-2\\text{ lines}),\\\\\n&\\text{horizontal line }y=1 \\quad (1\\text{ line}),\\\\\n&\\text{sunny line }S:\\;y = x-(n-3) \\quad (1\\text{ line}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(S\\) is sunny (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and it is distinct from the other lines.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le2\\).  \n  – For \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n  – For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(2 = (n-1)-(n-3)\\); hence \\((n-1,2)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines, and exactly one line (the line \\(S\\)) is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFrom (2) we have the universal upper bound \\(k\\le1\\).  \nSections 2 and 3 exhibit constructions achieving \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \nConsequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
