  Because \\(h+d+s=n-v=1\\), we must have \\(n+1-i_0=1\\), i.e. \\(i_0=n\\).  \n   Thus the only column without a vertical line is the right‑most column \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\), which contains a single point.  \n   That point can be covered either by a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny) or by a sunny line.  \n   Consequently the number of sunny lines can be either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).\n\n6. **Realising the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n   All lines are non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{vertical lines }x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 \\quad (n-2\\text{ lines}),\\\\\n   &\\text{horizontal line }y=1 \\quad (1\\text{ line}),\\\\\n   &\\text{sunny line }S:\\;y = x-(n-3) \\quad (1\\text{ line}).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(S\\) is sunny (its slope differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n   * If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n     – For \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n     – For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(2=(n-1)-(n-3)\\), so \\((n-1,2)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n   * If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 4 shows that no covering can contain two or more sunny lines; the constructions above show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are both attainable.  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
