ny two of these lie on a horizontal line (\\(y=1\\)) or on a line of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=3\\)), both non\u2011sunny.  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, requiring three sunny lines \u2013 impossible.\n\n*If \\(c=2\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAgain any pair among them is horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\); a sunny line can cover at most one point, so at least four sunny lines would be needed \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((3,1)\\) only.  \nThe five points left cannot be covered by two sunny lines for the same reason as in the horizontal case.\n\nThus a vertical non\u2011sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Case 3 \u2013 \\(N\\) of slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\))\n\nHere \\(N\\) has the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nThe possible values of \\(c\\) that intersect \\(S\\) are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n*If \\(c=2\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) only.  \nThe five remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines because any sunny line can contain at most two of them, and a covering of five points would need at least three sunny lines \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) has slope \\(1\\) \u2013 it is sunny.  \nThe two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=4\\), which is again of slope \\(-1\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nThus we would need a second non\u2011sunny line, contradicting the requirement of exactly one.\n\n*If \\(c=4\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny pair among these three points is horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so at lea