"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le 4\\) and contains exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines. Hence the required configuration does **not** exist.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. List the six points that must be covered:  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n2. Because exactly one of the three lines may be non\u2011sunny, enumerate the three possible directions of a non\u2011sunny line:  \n   * horizontal (\\(y=c\\)),  \n   * vertical (\\(x=c\\)),  \n   * slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n3. For each possible direction, consider every line of that direction that can contain points of \\(S\\).  \n   * Show that after removing the points covered by the chosen non\u2011sunny line, the remaining points cannot be covered by the two remaining sunny lines (each sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   * The obstruction is that any pair of the remaining points either lie on a horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\) line, i.e. on a non\u2011sunny line, so a sunny line can contain **at most one** of those points.  \n   * Consequently at least three sunny lines would be needed, contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.\n4. Since all possibilities lead to a contradiction, the desired configuration does not exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{ and }k=2\\text{ such a configuration does not exist.}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on the three lines.  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nWe are asked whether there exist three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(L_i\\);  \n* exactly two of the \\(L_i\\) are sunny (hence exactly one is non\u2011sunny).\n\nDenote the unique non\u2011sunny line by \\(N\\).  \nAccording to the definition of \u201cnon\u2011sunny\u201d, \\(N\\) must be of one of the following three types:\n\n1. **Horizontal:** \\(N\\colon y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\);\n2. **Vertical:** \\(N\\colon x=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\);\n3. **Slope \\(-1\\):** \\(N\\colon x+y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\).\n\nWe treat the three cases separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Case 1 \u2013 \\(N\\) horizontal\n\nThe only possible values of \\(c\\) that contain points of \\(S\\) are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n*If \\(c=1\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nAny two of these three points lie either on a vertical line (\\(x=1\\)) or on a line of slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line (which cannot have slope \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) can contain **at most one** of them.  Covering three points would require at least three sunny lines, contradicting the requirement of exactly two.\n\n*If \\(c=2\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAgain any pair among these points is horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so at least four sunny lines would be needed \u2013 impossible.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,3)\\) only.  \nThe five points left cannot be covered by two sunny lines because a sunny line can contain at most two of them (the only collinear triples have prohibited slopes).  Hence at least three sunny lines would be necessary \u2013 contradiction.\n\nThus a horizontal non\u2011sunny line cannot work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Case 2 \u2013 \\(N\\) vertical\n\nPossible values are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n*If \\(c=1\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAny two of these lie on a horizontal line or on a line of slope \\(-1\\); a sunny line can therefore contain at most one point, requiring three sunny lines \u2013 impossible.\n\n*If \\(c=2\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAll pairs are horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\); again a sunny line can cover at most one point, so at least four sunny lines would be needed \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((3,1)\\) only.  \nThe situation is identical to the sub\u2011case \\(c=3\\) of the horizontal case and leads to the same impossibility.\n\nHence a vertical non\u2011sunny line is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Case 3 \u2013 \\(N\\) of slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\))\n\nHere \\(N\\) has the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nThe possible values of \\(c\\) that intersect \\(S\\) are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n*If \\(c=2\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) only.  \nThe five remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines because a sunny line can contain at most two of them; covering five points would need at least three sunny lines \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe only sunny line that passes through two of these points is the line of slope \\(1\\) through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \nThe other two points, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), lie on the line \\(x+y=4\\), which has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  Consequently we would need a second non\u2011sunny line, contradicting the requirement that exactly one line be non\u2011sunny.\n\n*If \\(c=4\\):* \\(N\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny pair among them is horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\); thus a sunny 