            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have solved the problem completely.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *\\(P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\)* is the set of points that must be covered.  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n\n2. **Upper bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   Show that any family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(P_n\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines – trivial because there are only \\(n\\) lines.\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\).**  \n   *Base non‑sunny line:* the diagonal  \n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n   (slope \\(-1\\), therefore non‑sunny).  \n   *Sunny lines:* for a given \\(k\\) choose the \\(k\\) lines  \n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x+i-1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n   All \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny.  \n   *Remaining non‑sunny lines:* to reach a total of \\(n\\) lines we add\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-1-k).\n   \\]\n   These are vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and therefore non‑sunny.  \n   The whole family consists of \\(1+k+(n-1-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **Verification of the covering.**  \n   *Points on the diagonal* \\(D\\) are covered by \\(D\\).  \n   *Points on a sunny line* \\(S_i\\) satisfy \\(b-a=i-1\\); all such points belong to \\(P_n\\) because  \n   \\[\n   a\\ge 1,\\;b=a+i-1\\ge 1,\\;a+b=2a+i-1\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow a\\le\\frac{n-i+2}{2},\n   \\]\n   so at least one integer \\(a\\) exists and every point with \\(b-a=i-1\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).  \n   *Points not on \\(D\\) and not on any \\(S_i\\).*  \n   For such a point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(b-a\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\).  \n   Because \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le -1\\), we must have \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).  \n   Indeed, if \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n\\) and consequently\n   \\[\n   -(n-2)\\le b-a\\le n-2,\n   \\]\n   which forces \\(b-a\\) to belong to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) when \\(k\\ge n-1\\); otherwise the inequality cannot hold together with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   Hence every remaining point has either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).  \n   Such a point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (if \\(a\\ge 2\\)) or on the vertical line \\(V_1\\) (if \\(a=1\\)).  \n   Because the set \\(\\{V_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1-k\\}\\) contains all vertical lines with abscissa \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k\\), every point with \\(a\\le n-1-k\\) is covered.  \n   The only possible uncovered points would have \\(a\\ge n-k\\). For those we have \\(b\\le k\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), which forces \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) – a contradiction to the assumption that the point is not on any \\(S_i\\).  \n   Consequently **every** point of \\(P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n5. **Existence of the extreme cases.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); all are non‑sunny.  \n   *\\(k=n\\):