antee** that \\(t\\le k-1\\); for small \\(k\\) the value \\(t\\) can be larger than \\(k-1\\).\n\n   - **Concrete counter\u2011example:**  \n     Take \\(n=5,\\;k=4\\).  \n     The point \\((a,b)=(1,2)\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge n-k=1\\) and \\(a+b=3\\le n\\). Here \\(t=a-b=-1\\), which is not in \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\). The point \\((1,2)\\) is not on any of the constructed lines (vertical lines are absent because \\(n-k-1=0\\), the diagonal does not contain it, and none of the sunny lines \\(y=x-t\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\) pass through it). Hence the construction **fails to cover** this point.\n\n   Because the covering argument collapses for a whole class of points, the claim that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers all of \\(P_n\\) is false. This is a **critical error** that invalidates the entire construction for most values of \\(k\\) (in particular for any \\(k > n/2\\)). (Finding\u202f1)\n\n---\n\n### 4. Edge Cases\n\n#### \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201c*The construction gives the family \\(\\{D\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\), which are \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and clearly cover \\(P_n\\).*\u201d\n\n*Correct:* For \\(k=0\\) the vertical lines exist for all \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and together with the diagonal they indeed cover every point of \\(P_n\\). No error here.\n\n#### \\(k=n\\)\n\n> \u201c*Here the set of vertical lines is empty; we have the diagonal \\(D\\) and the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\). Since the diagonal is already counted, we may discard any one of the sunny lines to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\); the remaining \\(n\\) lines still cover \\(P_n\\).*\u201d\n\n**Problems:**\n\n1. **Number of lines:** Before discarding, the construction yields **\\(n+1\\) lines** (the diagonal plus \\(n\\) sunny lines). The requirement is exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the construction already violates the problem statement.\n\n2. **Sunny\u2011line count after discarding:** Removing a sunny line reduces the number of sunny lines to \\(n-1\\), contradicting 